  <tr><td>Section 21 POCSO</td><td>Punishment for failure to report or record — enhanced for persons in charge of companies and institutions</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>How the Zero FIR travels</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Information received anywhere</strong> — any police station must record it; jurisdiction objections are legally irrelevant at this stage.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Registered as "0"</strong> — the FIR is numbered zero, preserving the date, time and first version of the information.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Transferred</strong> — the file moves to the police station with territorial jurisdiction, which re-registers it under its own serial number.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Investigation proceeds</strong> — by the jurisdictional station and, in child sexual offence cases, with the SJPU's involvement and POCSO's child-specific protocols.</div>
</div>
<p>For child victims the value is evidentiary as much as humane: the earliest recorded account, before memory is worked over by time and family deliberation, anchors the prosecution case — and the registration timestamp defeats later defence arguments built on delay that the police themselves caused.</p>

<h2>Why these rules matter most in POCSO cases</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><h4>Mobility of the offence</h4><p>Children are abused in homes, schools, transport and online; the family often discovers the offence far from where it occurred. Zero FIR lets the report be made where the child is.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h4>Online offences</h4><p>Grooming, sexual harassment and child sexual abuse material under Sections 11-15 POCSO and the Information Technology Act, 2000 have no meaningful "place" at all; electronic reporting through the cyber crime portal and e-FIR fits the offence.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h4>The reluctance problem</h4><p>Institutional offenders — schools, hostels, shelters — generate pressure not to report. Section 21's institutional liability and the universal reporting duty are designed to break exactly that silence.</p></div>
</div>

<h2>The first 72 hours: a practical sequence</h2>
<div class="check">
  <ul>
    <li>Report at the nearest police station or to the SJPU — insist on registration citing Section 173(1) BNSS if jurisdiction is raised; note the names of officers spoken to.</li>
    <li>Ensure the child\'s medical examination and emergency medical care proceed immediately; care cannot be refused for want of an FIR.</li>
    <li>Ask that the child\'s statement be recorded per POCSO protocols — at the child\'s residence or place of choice, by an officer not in uniform, with a support person or parent present.</li>
    <li>If registration is refused, escalate the same day under Section 173(4) BNSS to the DCP, and thereafter to the Magistrate under Section 175(3).</li>
    <li>Preserve electronic evidence — chats, images, platform details — without forwarding or deleting, for seizure and certification in the investigation.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<h2>For the defence side, a note of balance</h2>
<p>The same provisions shape defence scrutiny: whether the Zero FIR\'s timestamp and transfer trail are intact, whether the recording safeguards were observed, whether the electronic information was signed within the statutory window, and whether the child\'s statements were recorded by the mandated procedure — departures go to the weight of the prosecution\'s earliest evidence. The registration rules protect the integrity of the process in both directions; that is what makes them worth knowing precisely.</p>
<div class="note">
  <p>This article describes the statutory reporting and registration framework in general terms for information. It is not legal advice, and cases involving children call for immediate professional assistance.</p>
</div>
